Build it with polling, not push — this platform's `api/` functions can't hold a WebSocket or SSE connection open: `PLATFORM.md` says connection upgrades return 501 at the edge, and instances freeze between invocations so a timer started during a request wouldn't survive anyway.

Since `rollup` only changes once a minute (`jobs/rollup.md`), polling faster than that just re-fetches the same `computed_at`. In `public/dashboard.js`, replace the click-only wiring with an interval that calls the same `load()`, and skip the re-render when `computed_at` hasn't changed since last poll:

```js
let lastComputedAt = null;

async function load() {
  const res = await fetch('/api/metrics');
  const { rows, computed_at } = await res.json();
  if (computed_at === lastComputedAt) return;
  lastComputedAt = computed_at;
  render(rows);
  document.getElementById('stamp').textContent = new Date(computed_at).toLocaleTimeString();
}

window.addEventListener('load', load);
setInterval(load, 20000);
```

The fork that matters: poll interval. 15–30s gives sub-minute staleness without meaningfully more load than the rollup job's own cadence; going below ~10s buys nothing since the source only updates once a minute. `/api/metrics` is already cheap (4KB, 30ms) so this doesn't need caching or a diff endpoint.

Left out: page visibility handling (pausing the interval when the tab is backgrounded) — worth adding if this runs on shared screens or laptops, skip it if it's just a monitor in the office.
